{"id": 41, "ts": 1780808956.3321407, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 3, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I continue to recommend **Candidate\u202fC**.\u202fWhile we have not yet explored Candidate\u202fA\u2019s reliability or stress resilience, the only facts we have are that A \u201canticipates dangerous situations,\u201d has \u201cexcellent spatial vision,\u201d and \u201cvery good leadership qualities,\u201d but also is \u201cunfriendly\u201d and \u201ceats unhealthily.\u201d\u202fWithout evidence of consistent operational reliability, stress handling, or crew cooperation, those leadership strengths cannot outweigh the interpersonal concerns.\nCandidate\u202fD shows strong problem\u2011solving (\u201csolves problems extremely well\u201d) and responsibility (\u201ctakes responsibility seriously\u201d), yet we have no concrete observations of his crew\u2011cooperation or professional communication, and the notes describe him as \u201cmoody,\u201d a \u201cloner,\u201d and having a \u201chot temper.\u201d\u202fThese unknowns and negative social descriptors leave a significant gap in the crew\u2011resource\u2011management criteria.\nBy contrast, Candidate\u202fC demonstrably meets the core safety pillars: \u201ccan make correct decisions quickly,\u201d stays \u201ccalm in a crisis,\u201d and has \u201cexcellent attention skills,\u201d directly covering decision quality, stress resilience, and attention accuracy.\u202fAlthough C \u201chas difficulty communicating ideas\u201d and is \u201cregarded as egocentric,\u201d the same notes state that C \u201cputs concern for others above everything,\u201d suggesting a willingness to prioritize team safety\u2014an attitude that can be reinforced through training.\u202fGiven the current evidence, C remains the strongest overall fit."}
